<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=soap">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Chemistry</p><h1 class="enc-title">Soap</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 2800 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/29th-century-bce">29th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Soap is a salt of a fatty acid, often made by mixing fats or oils with an alkali, used for washing and cleaning; the earliest recorded evidence of soap-like materials comes from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>, c. 2800 BCE. A Sumerian clay tablet of c. 2500 BCE gives a formula of oil and wood ash for washing woollen clothing. These proto-soaps differed from true soap, which foams; who first made true soap is uncertain, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pliny-the-elder">Pliny the Elder</a> called it &quot;an invention of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gauls">Gauls</a>&quot;. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islamic-golden-age">Islamic Golden Age</a> soap-making became an established industry.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/soap.jpg" alt="Soap" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: DimiTalen · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bars_of_pure_Marseille_and_Aleppo_soap,_2024.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Toilet soaps are made by saponification, in which an alkaline solution, often lye, breaks down the triglycerides of vegetable or animal oils and fats into salts of fatty acids and frees glycerol. Sodium soaps, made with soda lye, are firm, while potassium soaps, made with potash lye, are softer or often liquid. Soap made from pure <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olive-oil">olive oil</a> is sometimes called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> soap or Marseille soap. Soap cleans by gathering oil and dirt into tiny spheres called micelles, which the water then carries away.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Proto-soaps, which mixed fat and alkali for cleansing, are mentioned in Sumerian, Babylonian and Egyptian texts. The Ebers papyrus of about 1550 BCE shows that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptians">Egyptians</a> made a soap-like product as a medicine from animal fats or vegetable oils and trona. True soaps differed from these: they foamed, were made deliberately and could be hard or soft, because their makers understood sources of lye. It is uncertain who invented true soap, which may have arisen independently in various societies.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> cleaned the body by rubbing oil into the skin and scraping it off with a strigil, and they did not use soap until at least the first century CE. Pliny the Elder describes soap as an invention of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gauls">Gauls</a>, made from tallow and ashes. In the second century CE the physician <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galen">Galen</a> described soap-making with lye and rated Germanic soaps the best and those of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> second.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islamic-golden-age">Islamic Golden Age</a> hard, pleasantly scented toilet soap was produced in West Asia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a>, and soap-making became an established industry. Muhammad ibn Zakariya al-Razi gave recipes, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> soap was made from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olive-oil">olive oil</a>, alkali and lime and exported to the rest of the Muslim world and to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> the soapmakers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> formed a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guild</a> in the late sixth century, and by the 15th century soap was made on an industrial scale in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antwerp">Antwerp</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>, Marseille, Naples and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> a soap tax kept soap a luxury until it was repealed in 1853. Industrially made bar soaps became available in the late 18th century, and William Hesketh Lever and his brother bought a soap works in Warrington in 1886, which became Lever Brothers and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unilever">Unilever</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Soap” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>c. 2800 BCE</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Mesopotamia</dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>Who first made true soap, as distinct from earlier soap-like mixtures, is uncertain; Pliny the Elder called it an invention of the Gauls.</p><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=soap">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
